 the roadmap describes cksum as computing POSIX CRC-32 checksums with optional algorithm selection, and sum as computing BSD or System V checksums. However, the project context doesn't include the actual SRD text itself—only the vision, architecture, roadmap, code status, and requirement states. To properly describe the tasks, I'll need to reference the SRD files so the stitch agent can read them and extract the specific requirements. I'm laying out the specific files the stitch agent needs to read and modify—the main implementation file, its test suite, and the shared utilities it depends on—then mapping each requirement to the corresponding SRD specification so the agent knows exactly what to implement. I'm realizing I need to be careful about inventing design decisions without seeing the actual SRD content—the architecture reference points me there, but I should only include what I can derive from what's documented. For the sum package, since it doesn't exist yet in the codebase, I'd need to propose a contract task first before any implementation work, but cksum already has existing code so it can go straight to implementation tasks. I'll skip the contract task for cmd/sum since the package_contracts section only includes pkg/ packages, not cmd/ packages. Now I'm finalizing my four tasks: modifying cksum for requirements R2.1 through R3.1, then R3.2 and R3.3 separately, and creating the new sum command for R1.1 through R1.4.\n\nThe last task will modify sum for R2.1, R2.2, R3.1, and R3.2. Since cmd/cksum and cmd/sum are independent packages, tasks 1 and 3 can run in parallel, but task 2 depends on task 1 (same file), and task 4 depends on task 2. Now I'm verifying the weight budget—assuming weight 1 for each requirement, task 0 totals 4, task 1 totals 2, and task 2 should total 4 as well, all within the limit.\n\nThe constraint about splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks creates a tension with the 4-task limit. Tests are embedded in the same file